foreign  [Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Friday October 14th I'm Julie Chang for  the Wall Street Journal filling in for  Zoe Thomas  Tick Tock has helped more than a few  songs become viral Mega hits heat Waves  by glass animals  [Music]  blinding Lights by the weekend  [Applause]  [Music]  and now tick tock's parent company bite  dance is looking to expand its music  streaming business  the Chinese tech company is negotiating  with music labels as it sets its sights  on more than a dozen new markets while  the US isn't included in this next phase  bite Dan says it wanted services to be  available globally the Wall Street  journal's Deputy media editor Jessica  tonkel has been speaking with people  familiar with the company's plans and  she joins us now hi Jessica thank you so  much for coming on the show again thanks  for having me so Jessica bite dance is  looking to expand its music streaming  service across the globe the service  isn't widely available everywhere some  might not even know that it exists what  does its current streaming service look  like now so currently by dance has its  music subscription streaming service  which is called rezo is in three markets  it's in India Indonesia and Brazil and  you can think about it similar to  Spotify it's what we call a freemium  service and that you can have some  limited music listening over just for  free and you have to listen to ads with  that and then there's also a  subscription tier that you can pay for  and the pricing obviously is different  depending on the market do we know why  bite dance became interested in music  streaming in the first place and owning  Tick Tock play a role you know it's a  Natural Evolution given that they own  Tick Tock you know if you go on Tick  Tock you see musical artists break out  all the time it's not a coincidence that  so many of the top songs on the  Billboard charts are also trending on  Tick Tock it's kind of a Natural  Evolution that the company would say hey  maybe we should look into this and try  to keep people on our platform rather  than having them learn about an artist  on Tick Tock and then go to YouTube or  go to Spotify to listen to all their  music  and why is bite dance looking to expand  that service now  I dance and Tick Tock for that matter  are looking at everything that they can  to try to keep people on Tick Tock so  that could be Commerce which we've also  reported that they've been looking at  and doing more with or it's music right  anything that keeps people there longer  is better for the app they don't want  people to spend a few seconds on Tick  Tock and then go anywhere else they want  Tick Tock to be the app that everyone  spends time on  so with those goals in mind what kind of  challenges can we expect bite dance to  face here so there are multiple  challenges when Spotify launched several  years ago it's music streaming service  there was a hunger from the music labels  for some sort of alternative to Napster  at the time today this is a very  competitive market right you have  Spotify you have apple music you have  Amazon you have YouTube so they're  coming into a very competitive market on  top of which they're going to have to  negotiate all of these deals with the  music labels to be able to offer a  subscription music service globally so  that is always complicated you know  people we've spoke to have said that the  music labels really feel that rezo which  is the existing service could do a  better job of getting people to pay for  the service because the music labels  only make money if people are actually  subscribing and so I'm sure this is  going to be a lengthy negotiation I mean  Spotify knows very well how complicated  these negotiations are and you know to  be fair music streaming is not an easy  business in general so they're looking  at this again as a way to keep people on  the app so it'll be interesting to see  kind of what they look at to make this  different than everything else that's  out there speaking of competition you  know you mentioned Spotify Apple music  YouTube Amazon will bite dance and you  know Tick Tock getting into this Market  impact their competitors in any way or  is it just a matter of adding another  player to the field I think that Tick  Tock is in a very strong position if  they can do this all and I think the  thing to watch out for is how is it  integrated with the tick tock app are  you going to be able to see what all  your friends are listening to and click  on it and then subscribe to that artist  music like how embedded is it going to  be how integrated is it going to be how  social will it be there are a lot of  things that they can do if they get it  all right but this is you know I would  say this being a global Service is a far  way out I mean I think this will come  out in a few more markets in the next  few years and then they'll keep adding  to it and adding to it  all right Jessica tonkel thank you so  much for being here with us thanks for  having me again  and that's it for Tech news briefing  this week our supervising producer is  Chris sinsley and I'm Julie Chang  filling in for our host Zoe Thomas  thanks for listening and have a great  weekend  [Music]  thank you

